{
  "requirement": "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Determination: true.** The implementation covers every obligation in the requirement, and the tests cover the main points of the design space. The only weaknesses are small gaps in the unit-test inputs.\n\n**Matching rule.** `src/definition_glob.rs` has a single shared implementation.\n- `matches` (lines 16–36) is a dynamic-programming matcher over the whole name. `*` matches zero or more bytes, including `.`, and every other byte must match literally. Because the table must end in the accepting state, a match always covers the complete name, never a prefix or substring.\n- `validate` (lines 1–14) rejects the empty glob. It also rejects any character outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-*]`. That set equals the valid-name alphabet defined by `valid_name` in `src/lib.rs`, with `*` added.\n\n**Every selection path uses it.**\n- Requirements evidence inputs: `InputSelector::resolve` in `src/inputs.rs` calls `validate` and `matches`, skips `Term` definitions, and errors when nothing matches. `validate_resolved_input` in `src/evidence.rs` and the `add-input --requirements` command both go through `resolve`.\n- CLI filters: `status_markdown_with_options` and `info_markdown` in `src/commands.rs` both call `validate` and `matches`.\n- Authoring tools: the `references` tool in `src/authoring/tools.rs` validates the selection. It uses `matches` for the selected definitions, the reference sites, and the anchors. The authoring `source_input` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` applies `matches` for access checks, and `resolve` validates the glob afterwards.\n- Both requirements and named examples match. All of these paths exclude only `Term` definitions.\n- I found no other glob or wildcard matching code in `src`, so every selection site that I found is marked `{impl}` for this requirement.\n\n**Tests.**\n- The unit test `wildcard_crosses_dot_segments_and_matches_the_whole_name` in `src/definition_glob.rs` covers four things:\n  - `*` crossing dots;\n  - a prefix not matching;\n  - the literal dot being required, so `foo.bar.*` does not match `foo.bar`;\n  - the empty glob and `foo[bar]` being rejected.\n- `commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors` in `tests/commands.rs` covers three things end to end:\n  - `info` and `status` reject `[` with an \"invalid definition glob\" error;\n  - an unmatched glob is reported as an error;\n  - `requirements.*ready` matches both `requirements.ready` and `requirements.xready` (the `*` matching zero and non-zero characters) but not `requirements.pending`.\n- Other tests exercise globs against examples and exact names:\n  - `status_and_info_accept_a_definition_selection` shows `requirements.c*` selecting an example.\n  - `verbose_status_lists_fixme_judgments_and_globs_scope_issue_groups` and `status_preserves_repository_wide_rust_findings_in_every_form` scope `status` to a single name.\n  - `references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs` in `src/authoring/tools.rs` exercises the authoring tool with an exact name, `requirements.*`, a missing name, an invalid name, and an empty selection.\n  - `add_input_modes_capture_outline_nodes_and_refresh_idempotently` uses `rules.*` as a requirements input and checks the recorded `matches`.\n- The one unit test does not directly assert `*` matching empty or a leading `*`. The CLI tests cover the empty-match case.\n\nNo FIXME or acknowledged gaps are present.",
  "inputs": [
    {
